where does philosophy  begin and end does it have  borders or boundaries in asking this  we're really asking not just about  philosophy itself  but about its relationship to an  other to generally some other  discipline some other activity some  other literature some other  commitment and way of living and there  have been a lot of  candidates throughout the years we're  going to talk briefly about  rhetoric early on in philosophy religion  science and history including  philosophies own  history and you know if you if you  read through much philosophy sooner or  later  you're going to run into people who want  to make distinctions  along these lines and say there's some  sort of  division some sort of distinction some  sort of border  some sort of boundary and on this side  lies philosophy  on this side lies the something else  and there's a lot of different ways of  construing this i will point out that  just as  there is no consensus definition of what  philosophy  is or could be or should be there is no  absolutely agreed upon distinction  so when you read people saying  philosophy  is here and theology is here and this is  the  just the way it is you've got to take  that with a little grain of salt and if  you look at history that will help you  to to some degree contextualize if not  necessarily  relativize and entirely throw out these  attempts  at providing dividing lines that are  hard and fast  and when we see attempts like this  one of the key questions we want to ask  right away is  all right you've got a division that  you're making here what's your criterion  for that  is there a single way of saying  what lies on this side what lies on this  side is it a complex  of different ideas different  distinctions that are overlapping  with each other well that's that's a  significant  question another question that we have  to ask is  who's providing these criteria did they  just  pop into our heads were they worked out  over time are they the result of  somebody  imposing by fiat or are they something  that every  every rational person ought to be able  to see  that never actually does happen by the  way is  is the distinction and the criteria  coming from the side of philosophy  is philosophy getting to self-define  itself in relation to its other  or is it coming from the other side and  philosophy takes those  cues from the other discipline or  activity or literature and says okay  i'll accept what you have to say or is  it kind of a mixture of both  and then we can ask another key question  is the relationship between philosophy  and this other  discipline supposed to be neutral  is it cooperative is it  antagonistic is it a case of  subordination and super ordination where  maybe philosophy  stands above that discipline and tells  it what to do  or where philosophy takes its cues  or should take its cues from that other  discipline or set of disciplines or  activity well these are  all important questions and these are  all  part of the picture whenever anybody  invokes something like  you know you got philosophy and then you  got theology and it's really easy to  tell the difference between the two of  them  you know when thomas aquinas brings up  the bible he's doing theology when he's  bringing up bearish level he's doing  philosophy  you can say well what's your warrant  what's your evidence what's your  assurance what's your argument  for using that criterion who told you  that was right  right that's a good valid question to  ask  and that is a philosophical question  so philosophy you know once it starts to  think about itself is always involved in  meta philosophical questions about its  relation  to other things let's talk about a few  of these  right so early on  you know if we think about plato and  there's also you know similar  relationship and discussion going on in  aristotle in cicero in others philosophy  gets counter posed to rhetoric  and often rhetoric gets lumped under  sophistry right  and the sophists were these people who  wandered around  claiming to teach a variety of different  things many of them were  older or contemporaries of socrates some  of them  only claim to teach rhetoric like  gorgeous some of them claim to teach all  sorts of other things  as well and philosophy is supposed to be  something  better than that philosophy is supposed  to  get us to real wisdom whereas the  sophists just have a pretense of wisdom  there's a line being drawn right and who  gets to draw that line  well it's the philosophers and we should  be a little bit  you know leery sometimes about being too  uncritically  accepting about what plato has to say  given that his own student  aristotle thought that it was important  to write a book called the  art of rhetoric and in that book argues  that  in book three of the art of rhetoric  that  rhetoric is very important because all  teaching involves some persuasion you  know attending to the audience  rhetoric could be done in a way that was  more informed by philosophy had a less  antagonistic relationship with  it interestingly at the same time that  aristotle  is teaching isocrates one of the great  rhetoricians  has his own school where he's teaching  rhetoric  in the same place as the same city  as aristotle and his these these people  are referencing  back and forth and so there's this long  tangled history cicero gets involved in  it  later on in the  renaissance and onward we see  other thinkers bringing up the  importance of rhetoric for  philosophy we can still make a  distinction between them  but perhaps the distinction is less of a  strict  border and more like  you know several people living in the  same house  and working with each other and  consulting each other from time to time  another really big one religion  in western philosophy what we're mostly  thinking about are the  abrahamic monotheistic faiths of judaism  christianity and islam  but we could apply this to other  conceptions of religion and it's not as  if the greeks and romans didn't have  their own  rights rituals entire mythologies and  understandings of religion which varied  from time to time there were  philosophical  understandings of religion that we find  in plato  in the stoics in a variety of other  thinkers going all the way into late  neoplatonism  and quite often this is different than  the religion that the ordinary person on  the street might be practicing  so already religion is kind of a messy  term just like philosophy  no strict agreed upon definition of it  but we can say that you know  at some points religion  and philosophy drew upon each other and  at other points could be  quite hostile to each other what we're  interested in is  the people that are saying well you've  got religion on one side  or faith or theology or pick whatever  other term you want  and then you've got philosophy on the  other side and it might be in terms of  the criterion of  this is a purely rational natural  activity  this is a somewhat irrational informed  by revelation activity  and that distinction you know you can  say well  again who gets to make that is that a  distinction on the side of religion is  that a distinction on the side of  philosophy  when we make these distinctions and we  try to see what fits into what side  what do we what do we make of previous  thinkers their literature their activity  how philosophy is understood as a  discipline does that mean that we have  to push  plato onto the side of religion because  he  references religious con concepts and  brings up myths  socrates himself refers to his dimon  you see how complex and complicated  this can get another one science  and here we're talking about in the  modern era  and really the late modern era because  philosophy  was understood as a science an apistame  right or a techne or you know a sophia  kind of wisdom  throughout the ages a skientia in in  latin  and the other sciences you know some of  them were you know  developing alongside philosophy like  mathematics or medicine  others came out of philosophy physics  was part of philosophy  psychology part of philosophy originally  sociology part of philosophy  originally even economics starting out  as household management  and then eventually becoming political  economy there are all these back and  forth things but  you know nowadays we think of the  sciences as as being their own sort of  thing and having their own scientific  method which  they very often don't operate according  to the textbook scientific method it's  way more complicated than that but this  is this became a going concern  how can we separate philosophy off  from these sciences like the natural  sciences  like the social sciences all these other  things that might figure into it some  things that maybe  maybe they are a science maybe they  aren't like linguistics  right some things involving technology  is there a nice dividing line  that we can draw some people thought of  philosophy  as essentially coordinating all of the  other sciences for example  positivists like herbert spencer  and august compt in the 19th century  others thought that the sciences really  ought to  you know be handling the real problems  and then philosophy handles the  leftovers like  aj air and other logical positivists  of that time and then some people think  that there should be  fruitful interaction between it there's  even something we call philosophy  of science where you know we think about  what is an experiment  how do we tell whether an experiment was  successful  or not is that part of science or is  that part of philosophy  and there's you know the verdict is kind  of out  when it comes to this strict dividing  line  another one that comes up within  philosophy has to do with history  some people practicing or teaching  philosophy will say  well you've got real philosophy over  here and then you've got the history of  philosophy over here  history of philosophy is basically about  what other people thought  back in the day and we try to get clear  about that but real philosophies over  here where we're doing something new  something innovative something that's  leaving behind that history  and then you know a lot of people within  the history of philosophy say  wow you really got it wrong that's why  your philosophy keeps reinventing wheels  that you know  descartes already handled or the stoics  already discussed or  plutarch who you never read discussed  right you're  spending a lot of time doing things that  you think are new that  aren't actually new maybe it'd be better  to be informed by the history  of philosophy maybe that's what real  philosophizing would consistent once  again we see  a dividing line being set that that  people don't really  agree upon so you know you notice that i  i'm saying here as well that narratives  and history  figure into this i think personally  that having studied you know quite a bit  within the history of philosophy and the  history of ideas  is very helpful for realizing that  although we can draw  lines between philosophy and other  disciplines or activities  these lines are typically rather  arbitrary  we may have criteria but we have to ask  where those criteria came from  and studying the history of the ideas  helps to answer that  we also need to look at the narratives  that help  us understand why people took these  positions so  for example you know why did people say  oh you know religion is over here  philosophy is over here perhaps one of  the narratives is  religion is irrational something that  we're going to leave behind  and philosophy will take its place this  is associated  with a period that we call the  enlightenment in  the west in which many many people were  working out ideas like that  and they were casting the medieval  period as the dark ages in which  faith ruled and philosophy could not  have its full sway  well those narratives may be accurate or  they may be  self-serving things that function as an  ideology for people so we need to  be rather careful about that this is not  to say that we can't make any  distinctions whatsoever between  philosophy and other activities  disciplines literatures uh but it is to  say that we should exercise a little bit  of  common sense a little bit of caution a  little bit of  judgment and we should be a bit  suspicious  about these very neat dividing lines  that people would like to make  oftentimes  within the philosophical literature  discipline activity itself  you

